WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
A grid is not decoration.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:06.640
It is a promise that things which
look aligned really are aligned,

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:10.120
at every screen width, and
with every length of content.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:13.380
Count the distinct left edges on a screen.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:18.420
Reducing them is often the single biggest
improvement you can make to a busy layout.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.000
A dashboard with a heading,
a filter bar, three cards,

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.320
and a table might have seven different
left edges if each was placed by eye.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:30.180
Each edge is a small visual event.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:36.000
Reducing them to two or three calms the screen
more than a new colour or a new typeface.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:40.140
Twelve columns divide neatly
into halves, thirds, quarters,

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:43.600
and sixths, which is why
the number is so common.

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.060
You do not need to use all twelve.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:51.520
Most product screens use a few spans:
full width, two thirds plus one third,

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.240
or three equal cards.

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:57.260
The page is a 12 column grid, gap 24,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.200
which is on the spacing
scale from the last lesson.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:06.480
Max width 1200, centred, with
16 pixels of inline padding.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:10.160
Main spans 8. The aside spans 4.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:16.680
Below 768 pixels both span the full
grid, from line 1 to the last line.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:21.680
Use minmax 0, 1fr, not plain 1fr.

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:23.520
Without the zero minimum,

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:28.960
a long URL or a code block inside a column
can force that column wider than its

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:30.840
share and break the grid.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:35.140
1fr has an automatic
minimum based on content.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:41.160
minmax of 0 and 1fr lets the column
shrink so the grid stays even.

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:45.040
Before: a profile header
where the avatar, the name,

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.480
and the action button are each
centred in their own flex box.

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:54.940
Text baselines do not line up, and the button
floats a few pixels lower than the name.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.260
After: one row with align-items
center for the avatar,

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:03.020
and a shared baseline for the
name and the button text.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:08.560
Gap 16 between avatar and text,
gap 8 inside the meta row.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:12.820
The name and the button label now
sit on the same invisible line,

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.100
which is what the eye checks first.

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.800
Sometimes mathematically
aligned things look wrong.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:24.440
A play icon centred in a circle looks shifted
left because its visual weight sits on

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:29.380
one side. Round shapes look smaller
than squares of the same size.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.960
Nudge these by a pixel or two.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:38.080
Align to what people see, then leave a
comment so the next person does not undo it.

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:42.400
Count the left edges and remove the
ones you did not mean to create.

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:46.980
Use a column grid with a small
set of spans, and minmax 0,

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:50.180
1fr to keep columns honest.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.860
Align text on baselines, and trust
your eye for icons and round shapes.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:59.260
The next course takes the
same discipline into type:

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.780
a short scale of sizes, named by role,
so you stop inventing font sizes.
